78
00:06:14,400 --> 00:06:18,558
Stick to the boat, Pip, or by the Lord, I won't pick you up if you jump. Mind

79
00:06:18,559 --> 00:06:23,435
that we can't afford to lose whales by the likes of you. A whale would sell for

80
00:06:23,436 --> 00:06:28,479
30 times what you would, Pip, in Alabama. bear that in mind and don't

81
00:06:28,480 --> 00:06:34,479
jump anymore. Hereby perhaps Stub indirectly hinted that though man loved

82
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:39,755
his fellow, yet man is a money-making animal, which propensity too often

83
00:06:39,756 --> 00:06:42,634
interferes with his benevolence.

84
00:06:42,635 --> 00:06:48,079
But we are all in the hands of the gods," and Pip jumped again. It was

85
00:06:48,080 --> 00:06:52,238
under very similar circumstances to the first performance, but this time he did

86
00:06:52,239 --> 00:06:57,198
not breast out the line, and hence, when the whale started to run, Pip was left

87
00:06:57,199 --> 00:07:03,274
behind on the sea like a hurried traveler's trunk. Alas, Stub was but too

88
00:07:03,275 --> 00:07:08,479
true to his word. It was a beautiful bounteous blue day. The spangled sea

89
00:07:08,480 --> 00:07:14,159
calm and cool and flatly stretching away all round to the horizon like

90
00:07:14,160 --> 00:07:19,039
goldbeater's skin hammered out to the extremist. Bobbing up and down in that

91
00:07:19,040 --> 00:07:24,558
sea. Pip's eban head showed like a head of cloves. No boat knife was lifted when

92
00:07:24,559 --> 00:07:30,399
he fell so rapidly a stern. Stub's inexurable back was turned upon him, and

93
00:07:30,400 --> 00:07:35,198
the whale was winged. In 3 minutes a whole mile of shoreless ocean was

94
00:07:35,199 --> 00:07:40,954
between Pip and Stub. Out of the center of the sea, poor Pip turned his crisp,

95
00:07:40,955 --> 00:07:48,795
curling black head to the sun. Another lonely castaway, though the loftiest and the brightest.

96
00:07:48,796 --> 00:07:54,079
Now in calm weather, to swim in the open ocean is as easy to the practice swimmer

97
00:07:54,080 --> 00:07:57,039
as to ride in a spring carriage ashore.

98
00:07:57,040 --> 00:08:01,594
But the awful lonesomeness is intolerable. The intense concentration

99
00:08:01,595 --> 00:08:06,159
of self in the middle of such a heartless immensity. My God, who can

100
00:08:06,160 --> 00:08:12,079
tell it? Mark how when sailors in a dead calm bathe in the open sea, mark how

101
00:08:12,080 --> 00:08:17,838
closely they hug their ship and only coast along her sides. But had Stub

102
00:08:17,839 --> 00:08:23,274
really abandoned the poor little negro to his fate? No, he did not mean to at

103
00:08:23,275 --> 00:08:27,999
least because there were two boats in his wake, and he supposed no doubt that

104
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:32,558
they would, of course, come up to Pip very quickly and pick him up, though

105
00:08:32,559 --> 00:08:36,719
indeed such considerations towards oresman jeopardized through their own

106
00:08:36,720 --> 00:08:42,159
timidity, is not always manifested by the hunters in all similar instances,

107
00:08:42,160 --> 00:08:47,755
and such instances not unfrequently occur. Almost invariably in the fishery,

108
00:08:47,756 --> 00:08:53,115
a coward, so-called, is marked with the same ruthless detestation peculiar to

109
00:08:53,116 --> 00:08:55,999
military navies and armies.

110
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:00,238
But it so happened that those boats, without seeing Pip, suddenly spying

111
00:09:00,239 --> 00:09:05,755
whales close to them on one side, turned and gave chase, and Stub's boat was now

112
00:09:05,756 --> 00:09:11,518
so far away, and he and all his crew so intent upon the fish that Pip's ringed

113
00:09:11,519 --> 00:09:16,719
horizon began to expand around him miserably. By the merest chance, the

114
00:09:16,720 --> 00:09:21,274
ship itself at last rescued him. But from that hour the little negro went

115
00:09:21,275 --> 00:09:26,878
about the deck an idiot. Such at least they said he was. The sea had jeeringly

116
00:09:26,879 --> 00:09:32,399
kept his finite body up, but drowned the infinite of his soul. Not drowned

117
00:09:32,400 --> 00:09:37,914
entirely, though, rather carried down alive to wondrous depths, where strange

118
00:09:37,915 --> 00:09:42,795
shapes of the unwarped primal world glided to and fro before his passive

119
00:09:42,796 --> 00:09:48,878
eyes. and the miser merman wisdom revealed his hoarded heaps. And among

120
00:09:48,879 --> 00:09:54,238
the joyous, heartless, ever juvenile eternities, Pip saw the multitudinous

121
00:09:54,239 --> 00:09:59,518
god omnipresent coral insects that out of the firmament of waters heaved the

122
00:09:59,519 --> 00:10:05,914
colossal orbs. He saw God's foot upon the treddle of the loom and spoke it,

123
00:10:05,915 --> 00:10:11,435
and therefore his shipmates called him mad. So man's insanity is heaven's

124
00:10:11,436 --> 00:10:16,558
sense, and wandering from all mortal reason, man comes at last to that

125
00:10:16,559 --> 00:10:22,159
celestial thought, which to reason is absurd and frantic, and wheel or woe

126
00:10:22,160 --> 00:10:27,115
